<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Burna_Boy> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Damini Ebunoluwa Ogulu [MFR](:Member_of_the_Order_of_the_Federal_Republic)** (born 2 July 1991), known professionally as **Burna Boy**, is a Nigerian singer, songwriter and record producer. He rose to stardom in 2012 after releasing \"Like to Party\", the lead single from his debut studio album, *[L.I.F.E](:L%2EI%2EF%2EE)* (2013). In 2017, Burna Boy signed with Bad Habit/[Atlantic Records](:Atlantic_Records) in the United States and [Warner Music Group](:Warner_Music_Group) internationally. His third studio album, *[Outside](:Outside_%28Burna_Boy_album%29)*, marked his major-label debut.In 2019, he won Best International Act at the [2019 BET Awards](:BET_Awards_2019), and was announced as an [Apple Music Up Next](:Apple_Music_Up_Next) artist. His fourth studio album, *[African Giant](:African_Giant)*, was released in July 2019 and won Album of the Year at the 2019 [All Africa Music Awards](:All_Africa_Music_Awards) and was nominated for a [Best World Music Album](:Grammy_Award_for_Best_World_Music_Album) at the [62nd Annual Grammy Awards](:62nd_Annual_Grammy_Awards). He was awarded the African Artist of the year at the 2020 VGMA's.Burna Boy released his fifth studio album, *[Twice as Tall](:Twice_as_Tall)*, in August 2020, which was nominated for the same category at the [63rd Annual Grammy Awards](:63rd_Annual_Grammy_Awards), making him the first Nigerian artist with back-to-back nominations at the [Grammys.](:Grammy_Award) In 2021, *Twice as Tall* won the [Best World Music Album](:Grammy_Award_for_Best_World_Music_Album) at the [63rd Annual Grammy Awards](:63rd_Annual_Grammy_Awards).In July 2022, Burna Boy released his sixth studio album, *[Love, Damini](:Love%2C_Damini)* which also became the highest debut of an African album on *[Billboard](:Billboard_200)*, Netherlands, the United Kingdom and France. In October, Burna Boy was given the award of Member of the Order of the Federal Republic for his achievements in music."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
