<http://dbpedia.org/resource/London> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**London** is the capital and [largest city](:List_of_urban_areas_in_the_United_Kingdom) of [England](:England) and the [United Kingdom](:United_Kingdom), with a population of just under 9 million. It stands on the [River Thames](:River_Thames) in south-east England at the head of a 50-mile (80 km) [estuary](:Estuary) down to the [North Sea](:North_Sea), and has been a major settlement for two millennia. The [City of London](:City_of_London), its ancient core and [financial centre](:Financial_centre), was founded by the [Romans](:Roman_Empire) as *[Londinium](:Londinium)* and retains its medieval boundaries. The [City of Westminster](:City_of_Westminster), to the west of the City of London, has for centuries hosted the national [government](:Government_of_the_United_Kingdom) and [parliament](:Parliament_of_the_United_Kingdom). Since the 19th century, the name \"London\" has also referred to the [metropolis](:Metropolis) around this core, historically split between the [counties](:Counties_of_England) of [Middlesex](:Middlesex), [Essex](:Essex), [Surrey](:Surrey), [Kent](:Kent), and [Hertfordshire](:Hertfordshire), which largely comprises [Greater London](:Greater_London), governed by the [Greater London Authority](:Greater_London_Authority).As one of the world's major [global cities](:Global_cities), London exerts a strong influence on its arts, entertainment, [fashion](:Fashion_capital), commerce and finance, education, health care, [media](:Media_in_London), science and technology, [tourism](:Tourism_in_London), and transport and communications. Its GDP (€801.66 billion in 2017) makes it the largest [urban economy](:Urban_economy) in Europe, and it is one of the major financial centres in the world. With Europe's largest concentration of [higher education institutions](:List_of_universities_and_higher_education_colleges_in_London), it is home to some of the highest-ranked academic institutions in the world—[Imperial College London](:Imperial_College_London) in [natural](:Natural_sciences) and [applied sciences](:Applied_science), the [London School of Economics](:London_School_of_Economics) in [social sciences](:Social_science), and the comprehensive [University College London](:University_College_London). London is the [most visited city in Europe](:List_of_cities_by_international_visitors) and has the [busiest city airport system](:List_of_busiest_city_airport_systems_by_passenger_traffic) in the world. The [London Underground](:London_Underground) is the oldest [rapid transit](:Rapid_transit) system in the world. London is home to the most 5-star hotels of any city.London's diverse cultures encompass over 300 languages. The mid-2018 population of Greater London of about 9 million made it Europe's [third-most populous city](:List_of_European_cities_by_population_within_city_limits), accounting for 13.4% of the population of the United Kingdom and over 16% of the population of England. The [Greater London Built-up Area](:Greater_London_Built-up_Area) is the [fourth-most populous](:List_of_urban_areas_in_Europe) in Europe with about 9.8 million inhabitants at the 2011 census. The [London metropolitan area](:London_metropolitan_area) is the [third-most populous](:List_of_metropolitan_areas_in_Europe) in Europe with about 14 million inhabitants in 2016, granting London the status of a [megacity](:Megacity).London has four [World Heritage Sites](:World_Heritage_Site): the [Tower of London](:Tower_of_London); [Kew Gardens](:Kew_Gardens); the combined [Palace of Westminster](:Palace_of_Westminster), [Westminster Abbey](:Westminster_Abbey), and [St Margaret's Church](:St_Margaret%27s%2C_Westminster); and also the historic settlement in [Greenwich](:Greenwich), where the [Royal Observatory, Greenwich](:Royal_Observatory%2C_Greenwich) defines the [prime meridian](:Prime_meridian_%28Greenwich%29) (0° [longitude](:Longitude%29) and [Greenwich Mean Time](:Greenwich_Mean_Time). Other landmarks include [Buckingham Palace](:Buckingham_Palace), the [London Eye](:London_Eye), [Piccadilly Circus](:Piccadilly_Circus), [St Paul's Cathedral](:St_Paul%27s_Cathedral), [Tower Bridge](:Tower_Bridge), and [Trafalgar Square](:Trafalgar_Square). London has [many museums](:List_of_museums_in_London), galleries, libraries and cultural venues, including the [British Museum](:British_Museum), [National Gallery](:National_Gallery), [Natural History Museum](:Natural_History_Museum%2C_London), [Tate Modern](:Tate_Modern), [British Library](:British_Library), and numerous [West End](:West_End_theatre) theatres. Important sporting events held in London include the [FA Cup Final](:FA_Cup_Final) (held annually at [Wembley Stadium](:Wembley_Stadium%29), [Wimbledon Tennis Championships](:The_Championships%2C_Wimbledon) and the [London Marathon](:London_Marathon). In [2012](:2012_Summer_Olympics), London became the first city to host three [Summer Olympic Games](:Summer_Olympic_Games)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
