<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Osaka> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Osaka** ([Japanese](:Japanese_language): 大阪市, [Hepburn](:Hepburn_romanization): *Ōsaka-shi*, Template:IPA-ja; commonly just 大阪, *Ōsaka* Template:IPA-ja) is a [designated city](:Cities_designated_by_government_ordinance_of_Japan) in the [Kansai region](:Kansai_region) of [Honshu](:Honshu) in [Japan](:Japan). It is the capital of and most populous city in [Osaka Prefecture](:Osaka_Prefecture), and the [third most populous city](:List_of_cities_in_Japan) in Japan, following [Special wards of Tokyo](:Special_wards_of_Tokyo) and [Yokohama](:Yokohama). With a population of 2.7 million in the 2020 census, it is also the largest component of the [Keihanshin Metropolitan Area](:Keihanshin), which is the [second-largest metropolitan area in Japan](:List_of_metropolitan_areas_in_Japan) and the 10th [largest urban area](:List_of_urban_areas_by_population) in the world with more than 19 million inhabitants.Osaka was traditionally considered Japan's economic hub. By the [Kofun period](:Kofun_period) (300–538) it had developed into an important regional port, and in the 7th and 8th centuries, it served briefly as the imperial capital. Osaka continued to flourish during the [Edo period](:Edo_period) (1603–1867) and became known as a center of Japanese culture. Following the [Meiji Restoration](:Meiji_Restoration), Osaka greatly expanded in size and underwent rapid industrialization. In 1889, Osaka was officially established as a municipality. The construction boom accelerated population growth throughout the following decades, and by the 1900s, Osaka was the industrial hub in the [Meiji](:Meiji_%28era%29) and [Taishō](:Taish%C5%8D) periods. Osaka made noted contributions to redevelopment, urban planning and zoning standards in the postwar period, the city developed rapidly as one of the major financial center in the Keihanshin Metropolitan Area.Osaka is a major financial center of Japan, and it is recognized as one of the most [multicultural](:Multiculturalism) and [cosmopolitan](:Cosmopolitanism) cities in Japan. The city is home to the [Osaka Exchange](:Osaka_Exchange) as well as the headquarters of multinational [electronics](:Electronics) corporations such as [Panasonic](:Panasonic) and [Sharp](:Sharp_Corporation). Osaka is an international center of research and development and is represented by several major universities, notably [Osaka University](:Osaka_University), [Osaka Metropolitan University](:Osaka_Metropolitan_University), and [Kansai University](:Kansai_University). Famous landmarks in the city include [Osaka Castle](:Osaka_Castle), [Osaka Aquarium Kaiyukan](:Osaka_Aquarium_Kaiyukan), [Dōtonbori](:D%C5%8Dtonbori), [Tsūtenkaku](:Ts%C5%ABtenkaku) in [Shinsekai](:Shinsekai), [Tennōji Park](:Tenn%C5%8Dji_Park), [Abeno Harukas](:Abenobashi_Terminal_Building), [Sumiyoshi Taisha Grand Shrine](:Sumiyoshi_Taisha), and [Shitennō-ji](:Shitenn%C5%8D-ji), one of the oldest Buddhist temples in Japan."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
