<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Dickey_Betts> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Forrest Richard Betts (born December 12, 1943) is an American guitarist, singer, songwriter, and composer best known as a founding member of The Allman Brothers Band. Early in his career, he collaborated with Duane Allman, introducing melodic twin guitar harmony and counterpoint which \"rewrote the rules for how two rock guitarists can work together, completely scrapping the traditional rhythm/lead roles to stand toe to toe\". Following Allman's death in 1971, Betts assumed sole lead guitar duties during the peak of the group's commercial success in the mid-1970s."@en .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/University_of_St_Andrews> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "[St Mary's College](:St_Mary%27s_College%2C_St_Andrews)  [School of Medicine](:University_of_St_Andrews_School_of_Medicine)  [St Leonard's College](:St_Leonard%27s_College_%28University_of_St_Andrews%29)  The **University of St Andrews** (Template:Lang-sco, Template:Lang-gd; abbreviated as **St And**, from the [Latin](:Latin_language) *Sancti Andreae*, in [post-nominals](:Post-nominals%29) is a [public university](:Public_university) in [St Andrews, Fife](:St_Andrews%2C_Fife), Scotland. It is the [oldest](:List_of_oldest_universities_in_continuous_operation) of the four [ancient universities of Scotland](:Ancient_universities_of_Scotland) and, following the universities of [Oxford](:University_of_Oxford) and [Cambridge](:University_of_Cambridge), the third-oldest university in the [English-speaking world](:English-speaking_world). St Andrews was founded in 1413 when the [Avignon](:Avignon_Pope) [Antipope Benedict XIII](:Antipope_Benedict_XIII) issued a [papal bull](:Papal_bull) to a small founding group of [Augustinian](:Augustinians) clergy. Along with the universities of [Glasgow](:University_of_Glasgow), [Edinburgh](:University_of_Edinburgh), and [Aberdeen](:University_of_Aberdeen), St Andrews was part of the [Scottish Enlightenment](:Scottish_Enlightenment) during the 18th century.St Andrews is made up of a variety of institutions, comprising three colleges — [United College](:United_College%2C_St_Andrews) (a union of St Salvator's and St Leonard's Colleges), [St Mary's College](:St_Mary%27s_College%2C_St_Andrews), and [St Leonard's College](:St_Leonard%27s_College_%28University_of_St_Andrews%29), the last named being a non-statutory revival of St Leonard's as a post-graduate society. There are 18 academic schools organised into four faculties. The university occupies historic and modern buildings located throughout the town. The academic year is divided into two semesters, Martinmas and Candlemas. In term time, over one-third of the town's population are either staff members or students of the university. The student body is notably diverse: over 145 nationalities are represented with 45% of its intake from countries outside the UK; about one-eighth of the students are from the EU and the remaining third are from overseas—15% from North America alone. The university's sport teams compete in [BUCS](:BUCS) competitions, and the student body is known for preserving ancient traditions such as Raisin Weekend, May Dip, and the wearing of distinctive academic dress.It has been twice named \"[University of the Year](:Sunday_Times_University_of_the_Year)\" by *The Times and Sunday Times*' [Good University Guide](:Rankings_of_universities_in_the_United_Kingdom), one of only two UK universities to achieve this. In the 2022 Good University Guide, St Andrews was ranked as the best university in the UK, the first university to ever top Oxford and Cambridge in British rankings. In 2021, St Andrews had the highest entry standards for undergraduate admission in the UK, attaining an average UCAS Entry Tariff of 208 points. St Andrews has many notable alumni and affiliated faculty, including eminent mathematicians, scientists, theologians, philosophers, and politicians. Recent alumni include the former first minister of Scotland [Alex Salmond](:Alex_Salmond); Cabinet Secretary and head of the Civil Service [Mark Sedwill](:Mark_Sedwill); Chief of the Secret Intelligence Service (MI6) [Alex Younger](:Alex_Younger); former secretary of state for defence Sir [Michael Fallon](:Michael_Fallon); Olympic cycling gold medalist [Chris Hoy](:Chris_Hoy); Permanent Representative of the United Kingdom to the United Nations and former British Ambassador to China (2015–2020) Dame [Barbara Woodward](:Barbara_Woodward); and royals [William, Prince of Wales](:William%2C_Prince_of_Wales), and [Catherine, Princess of Wales](:Catherine%2C_Princess_of_Wales). Five Nobel laureates are among St Andrews' alumni and former staff: three in [Chemistry](:Nobel_Prize_in_Chemistry) and two in [Physiology or Medicine](:Nobel_Prize_in_Physiology_or_Medicine)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
