<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Indianapolis> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Indianapolis** (), colloquially known as **Indy**, is the [state capital](:List_of_U%2ES%2E_state_and_territorial_capitals) and [most-populous city](:List_of_U%2ES%2E_states%27_largest_cities_by_population) of the [U.S. state](:U%2ES%2E_state) of [Indiana](:Indiana) and the [seat](:County_seat) of [Marion County](:Marion_County%2C_Indiana). According to the [United States Census Bureau](:United_States_Census_Bureau), the consolidated population of Indianapolis and Marion County in 2020 was 977,203. The \"[balance](:Indianapolis_%28balance%29)\" population, which excludes semi-autonomous municipalities in Marion County, was 887,642. It is the [15th most populous](:List_of_United_States_cities_by_population) city in the U.S., the third-most populous city in the [Midwest](:Midwestern_United_States), after [Chicago](:Chicago) and [Columbus, Ohio](:Columbus%2C_Ohio), and the fourth-most populous state capital after [Phoenix, Arizona](:Phoenix%2C_Arizona); [Austin, Texas](:Austin%2C_Texas); and Columbus. The [Indianapolis metropolitan area](:Indianapolis_metropolitan_area) is the [33rd most populous](:List_of_Metropolitan_Statistical_Areas) [metropolitan statistical area](:Metropolitan_statistical_area) in the U.S., with 2,111,040 residents. Its [combined statistical area ranks 28th](:Combined_statistical_area), with a population of 2,431,361. Indianapolis covers 368 square miles (950 km2), making it the [18th largest city by land area](:List_of_United_States_cities_by_area) in the U.S.[Indigenous peoples](:Indigenous_peoples_of_the_Americas) inhabited the area dating to as early as 10,000 BC. In 1818, the [Lenape](:Lenape) relinquished their tribal lands in the [Treaty of St. Mary's](:Treaty_of_St%2E_Mary%27s). In 1821, Indianapolis was founded as a [planned city](:Planned_city) for the new seat of Indiana's state government. The city was [platted](:Plat) by [Alexander Ralston](:Alexander_Ralston) and [Elias Pym Fordham](:Elias_Pym_Fordham) on a 1-square-mile (2.6 km2) grid next to the [White River](:White_River_%28Indiana%29). Completion of the [National](:National_Road) and [Michigan](:Michigan_Road) roads and arrival of rail later solidified the city's position as a manufacturing and transportation hub. Two of the city's nicknames reflect its historical ties to transportation—the \"[Crossroads of America](:Crossroads_of_America)\" and \"Railroad City\". Since the 1970 [city-county consolidation](:City-county_consolidation), known as [Unigov](:Unigov), local government administration operates under the direction of an elected 25-member [city-county council](:Indianapolis_City-County_Council) headed by the [mayor](:List_of_mayors_of_Indianapolis).Indianapolis anchors the [29th largest economic region](:List_of_U%2ES%2E_metropolitan_areas_by_GDP) in the U.S., based primarily on the industries of trade, transportation, and utilities; professional and business services; education and health services; government; leisure and hospitality; and manufacturing. The city has notable [niche markets](:Niche_market) in [amateur sports](:Amateur_sports) and [auto racing](:Auto_racing). The city is home to three [*Fortune* 500](:Fortune_500) companies, two major league sports clubs ([Colts](:Indianapolis_Colts) and [Pacers](:Indiana_Pacers%29), five university campuses, and several museums, including [the world's largest children's museum](:The_Children%27s_Museum_of_Indianapolis). However, the city is perhaps best known for annually hosting the world's largest single-day sporting event, the [Indianapolis 500](:Indianapolis_500). Among the city's [historic sites and districts](:National_Register_of_Historic_Places_listings_in_Marion_County%2C_Indiana), Indianapolis is home to the largest collection of monuments dedicated to veterans and war casualties in the U.S. outside of [Washington, D.C.](:Washington%2C_D%2EC%2E)"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
