<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Beaux-Arts_de_Paris> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The **Beaux-Arts de Paris** is a French *[grande école](:Grande_%C3%A9cole)* whose primary mission is to provide high-level arts education and training. This is classical and historical School of [Fine Arts](:Fine_Arts) in France. The [art school](:Art_school), which is part of the [Paris Sciences et Lettres University](:Paris_Sciences_et_Lettres_University), is located on two sites: [Saint-Germain-des-Prés](:Saint-Germain-des-Pr%C3%A9s) in Paris, and [Saint-Ouen](:Saint-Ouen-sur-Seine).The Parisian institution is made up of a complex of buildings located at 14 rue Bonaparte, between the quai Malaquais and the [rue Bonaparte](:Rue_Bonaparte). This is in the heart of Saint-Germain-des-Prés, just across the Seine from the [Louvre](:Louvre) museum. The school was founded in 1648 by [Charles Le Brun](:Charles_Le_Brun) as the famed French academy *[Académie de peinture et de sculpture](:Acad%C3%A9mie_de_peinture_et_de_sculpture)*. In 1793, at the height of the [French Revolution](:French_Revolution), the institutes were suppressed. However, in 1817, following the [Bourbon Restoration](:Bourbon_Restoration_in_France), it was revived under a changed name after merging with the [Académie d'architecture](:Acad%C3%A9mie_royale_d%27architecture). Held under the King's tutelage until 1863, an imperial decree on November 13, 1863 named the school's director, who serves for a five-year term. Long supervised by the Ministry of Public Instruction, the École des Beaux-Arts is now a public establishment under the Ministry of Culture."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
