<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Gothenburg> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Gothenburg** ( ; abbreviated **Gbg**; Template:Lang-sv Template:IPA-sv is the [second-largest city](:List_of_urban_areas_in_Sweden_by_population) in [Sweden](:Sweden), fifth-largest in the [Nordic countries](:Nordic_countries), and [capital](:Capital_city) of the [Västra Götaland County](:V%C3%A4stra_G%C3%B6taland_County). It is situated by the [Kattegat](:Kattegat), on the west coast of Sweden, and has a population of approximately 590,000 in the [city proper](:City_proper) and about 1.1 million inhabitants in the [metropolitan area](:Metropolitan_Gothenburg).Gothenburg was founded as a heavily fortified, primarily Dutch, trading colony, by [royal charter](:Royal_charter) in 1621 by [King Gustavus Adolphus](:King_Gustavus_Adolphus). In addition to the generous privileges (e.g. tax relaxation) given to his Dutch allies from the ongoing [Thirty Years' War](:Thirty_Years%27_War), the king also attracted significant numbers of his [German](:German_people) and [Scottish](:Scottish_people) allies to populate his only town on the western coast. At a key strategic location at the mouth of the [Göta älv](:G%C3%B6ta_%C3%A4lv), where Scandinavia's largest drainage basin enters the sea, the [Port of Gothenburg](:Port_of_Gothenburg) is now the largest port in the Nordic countries.Gothenburg is home to many students, as the city includes the [University of Gothenburg](:University_of_Gothenburg) and [Chalmers University of Technology](:Chalmers_University_of_Technology). [Volvo](:Volvo) was founded in Gothenburg in 1927. The original parent Volvo Group and the now-separate [Volvo Car Corporation](:Volvo_Car_Corporation) are still headquartered on the island of [Hisingen](:Hisingen) in the city. Other key companies are [SKF](:SKF) and [AstraZeneca](:AstraZeneca).Gothenburg is served by [Göteborg Landvetter Airport](:G%C3%B6teborg_Landvetter_Airport) 25 km (16 mi) southeast of the city center. The smaller [Göteborg City Airport](:G%C3%B6teborg_City_Airport), 15 km (9.3 mi) from the city center, was closed to regular airline traffic in 2015.The city hosts the [Gothia Cup](:Gothia_Cup), the world's largest youth football tournament, and the [Göteborg Basketball Festival](https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Göteborg%20Basketball%20Festival), Europe's largest youth basketball tournament, alongside some of the largest annual events in Scandinavia. The [Gothenburg Film Festival](:Gothenburg_Film_Festival), held in January since 1979, is the leading Scandinavian film festival, with over 155,000 visitors each year. In summer, a wide variety of music festivals are held in the city, including the popular [Way Out West Festival](:Way_Out_West_Festival).During 2020, Gothenburg's population increased by 3,775 inhabitants."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
