<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Budapest> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Budapest** (, ; Template:IPA-hu is the [capital](:Capital_city) and [most populous city](:List_of_cities_and_towns_of_Hungary) of [Hungary](:Hungary). It is the [ninth-largest city](:List_of_cities_in_the_European_Union_by_population_within_city_limits) in the [European Union](:European_Union) by population within city limits and the [second-largest city](:List_of_cities_and_towns_on_Danube_river) on the [Danube](:Danube) river; the city has an estimated population of 1,752,286 over a land area of about 525 square kilometres (203 square miles). Budapest, which is both a [city](:List_of_cities_and_towns_of_Hungary) and [county](:Counties_of_Hungary), forms the centre of the [Budapest metropolitan area](:Budapest_metropolitan_area), which has an area of 7,626 square kilometres (2,944 square miles) and a population of 3,303,786; it is a [primate city](:Primate_city), constituting 33% of the population of Hungary.The [history of Budapest](:History_of_Budapest) began when an early [Celtic](:Celts) settlement transformed into the [Roman](:Ancient_Rome) town of [Aquincum](:Aquincum), the capital of [Lower Pannonia](:Pannonia_Inferior). The [Hungarians](:Hungarian_people) arrived in the territory in the late 9th century, but the area was pillaged by the [Mongols](:Mongol_invasion_of_Europe) in 1241–42. Re-established [Buda](:Buda) became one of the centres of [Renaissance humanist](:Renaissance_humanism) culture by the 15th century. The [Battle of Mohács](:Battle_of_Moh%C3%A1cs), in 1526, was followed by nearly 150 years of [Ottoman](:Ottoman_Empire) rule. After the [reconquest of Buda](:Siege_of_Buda_%281686%29) in 1686, the region entered a new age of prosperity, with Pest-Buda becoming a global city after the unification of Buda, [Óbuda](:%C3%93buda) and [Pest](:Pest%2C_Hungary) on 17 November 1873, with the name 'Budapest' given to the new capital. Budapest also became the co-capital of the [Austro-Hungarian Empire](:Austria-Hungary), a [great power](:Great_power) that dissolved in 1918, following [World War I](:World_War_I). The city was the focal point of the [Hungarian Revolution of 1848](:Hungarian_Revolution_of_1848), the [Battle of Budapest](:Battle_of_Budapest) in 1945, as well as the [Hungarian Revolution of 1956](:1956_Hungarian_Revolution).Budapest is a global city with strengths in commerce, finance, media, art, fashion, research, technology, education, and entertainment. Hungary's [financial centre](:Financial_centre), it is the second richest capital and city in the region after [Bucharest](:Bucharest). Budapest is the headquarters of the [European Institute of Innovation and Technology](:European_Institute_of_Innovation_and_Technology), the [European Police College](:European_Police_College) and the first foreign office of the [China Investment Promotion Agency](:China_Investment_Promotion_Agency). [Over 40 colleges and universities](:List_of_universities_in_Hungary) are located in Budapest, including the [Eötvös Loránd University](:E%C3%B6tv%C3%B6s_Lor%C3%A1nd_University), the [Corvinus University](:Corvinus_University), [Semmelweis University](:Semmelweis_University), [University of Veterinary Medicine Budapest](:University_of_Veterinary_Medicine_Budapest) and the [Budapest University of Technology and Economics](:Budapest_University_of_Technology_and_Economics). Opened in 1896, the city's subway system, the [Budapest Metro](:Budapest_Metro), serves 1.27 million, while the [Budapest Tram Network](:Trams_in_Budapest) serves 1.08 million passengers daily.The central area of Budapest along the [Danube River](:Danube) is classified as a [UNESCO](:UNESCO) [World Heritage Site](:World_Heritage_Site) and has several notable monuments of [classical architecture](:Classical_architecture), including the [Hungarian Parliament](:Hungarian_Parliament_Building) and the [Buda Castle](:Buda_Castle). The city also has around [80 geothermal springs](:Sz%C3%A9chenyi_Medicinal_Bath), the largest thermal water cave system, second largest [synagogue](:Doh%C3%A1ny_Street_Synagogue), and third largest [Parliament](:Hungarian_Parliament_Building) building in the world. Budapest [attracts](:Tourism_in_Hungary) around 12 million international tourists per year, making it a highly popular destination in Europe. It also topped the *Best European Destinations 2020* list by Big7Media. Budapest also ranks as the third-best European city in a similar poll conducted by *[Which?](:Which%3F)*."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
