<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Memphis,_Tennessee> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Memphis** is a city in the [U.S. state](:U%2ES%2E_state) of [Tennessee](:Tennessee). It is the [seat](:County_seat) of [Shelby County](:Shelby_County%2C_Tennessee) in the southwest part of the state; it is situated along the [Mississippi River](:Mississippi_River). With a population of 633,104 at the [2020 U.S. census](:2020_United_States_census), Memphis is the [second-most populous city](:List_of_municipalities_in_Tennessee) in Tennessee, after [Nashville](:Nashville%2C_Tennessee).Memphis is the fifth-most populous city in the [Southeast](:Southeastern_United_States), the nation's [28th-largest](:List_of_United_States_cities_by_population) overall, as well as the largest city bordering the Mississippi River. The [Memphis metropolitan area](:Memphis_metropolitan_area) includes [West Tennessee](:West_Tennessee) and the greater [Mid-South region](:Mid-South_%28region%29), which includes portions of neighboring [Arkansas](:Arkansas), [Mississippi](:Mississippi) and the [Missouri Bootheel](:Missouri_Bootheel). One of the more historic and culturally significant cities of the [Southern United States](:Southern_United_States), Memphis has a wide variety of landscapes and [distinct neighborhoods](:List_of_neighborhoods_in_Memphis%2C_Tennessee).The first European explorer to visit the area of present-day Memphis was Spanish [conquistador](:Conquistador) [Hernando de Soto](:Hernando_de_Soto) in 1541. The high [Chickasaw Bluffs](:Chickasaw_Bluff) protecting the location from the waters of the Mississippi was contested by Spanish, French, and English colonizers as Memphis developed. By 1819, when modern Memphis was founded, it was part of United States territory. [John Overton](:John_Overton_%28judge%29), [James Winchester](:James_Winchester_%28general%29), and [Andrew Jackson](:Andrew_Jackson) founded the city. Based on the wealth of cotton plantations and river traffic along the Mississippi, Memphis grew into one of the largest cities of the [Antebellum South](:Antebellum_South). After the [American Civil War](:American_Civil_War) and the end of [slavery](:Slavery_in_the_United_States), the city continued to grow into the 20th century. It became among the largest world markets for [cotton](:Cotton) and [lumber](:Lumber).Home to Tennessee's largest [African-American](:African_American) population, Memphis played a prominent role in the [American Civil Rights Movement](:American_Civil_Rights_Movement). Leader [Martin Luther King Jr.](:Martin_Luther_King_Jr%2E) was [assassinated there in 1968](:Assassination_of_Martin_Luther_King_Jr%2E) after activities supporting a strike by the city's maintenance workers. The [National Civil Rights Museum](:National_Civil_Rights_Museum) was established there and is a [Smithsonian](:Smithsonian_Institution) affiliate institution.Since the civil rights era, Memphis has become one of the nation's leading commercial centers in transportation and [logistics](:Logistics). The largest employer is [FedEx](:FedEx), which maintains its global air hub at [Memphis International Airport](:Memphis_International_Airport). Since the USSR collapsed in 1991, Memphis has superseded Anchorage as the [busiest cargo airport in the world](:List_of_busiest_airports_by_cargo_traffic). The [International Port of Memphis](:Port_of_Memphis) also hosts the fifth-busiest inland water port in the U.S. The [Globalization and World Cities Research Network](:Globalization_and_World_Cities_Research_Network) considers Memphis a \"Sufficiency\" level [global city](:Global_city) as of 2020.Memphis is a center for media and entertainment, notably a historic [music scene](:Music_of_Tennessee). With [blues](:Blues) clubs on [Beale Street](:Beale_Street) originating the unique [Memphis blues](:Memphis_blues) sound, the city has been nicknamed the \"Home of the Blues\". Its music has continued to be shaped by a multicultural mix of influences: [country](:Country_music), [rock and roll](:Rock_and_roll), [soul](:Memphis_soul), and [hip-hop](:Memphis_rap).The city is home to a [major professional sports team](:Major_professional_sports_leagues_in_the_United_States_and_Canada), the [Grizzlies](:Memphis_Grizzlies) of the [NBA](:NBA). Other attractions include [Graceland](:Graceland), the [Memphis Pyramid](:Memphis_Pyramid), [Sun Studio](:Sun_Studio), the [Blues Hall of Fame](:Blues_Hall_of_Fame) and [Stax Museum of American Soul Music](:Stax_Museum_of_American_Soul_Music). [Memphis-style barbecue](:Memphis-style_barbecue) has achieved international prominence, and the city hosts the annual [World Championship Barbecue Cooking Contest](:Memphis_in_May%23World_Championship_Barbecue_Cooking_Contest_%28WCBCC%29), which attracts more than 100,000 visitors each year. Higher-level educational institutions include the [University of Memphis](:University_of_Memphis) and [Rhodes College](:Rhodes_College)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
