<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Peterborough,_Ontario> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Peterborough** ( [*PEE-tər-burr-oh*](:Help%3APronunciation_respelling_key%29) is a city on the [Otonabee River](:Otonabee_River) in [Ontario](:Ontario), Canada, about 125 kilometres (78 miles) northeast of [Toronto](:Toronto). According to the 2021 Census, the population of the City of Peterborough was 83,651. The population of the Peterborough Census Metropolitan Area (CMA), which includes the surrounding Townships of [Selwyn](:Selwyn%2C_Ontario), [Cavan Monaghan](:Cavan_Monaghan), [Otonabee-South Monaghan](:Otonabee-South_Monaghan), and [Douro-Dummer](:Douro-Dummer), was 128,624 in 2021. In 2021, Peterborough ranked 32nd among the country's 41 census metropolitan areas according to the [CMA in Canada](:List_of_census_metropolitan_areas_and_agglomerations_in_Canada). The current [mayor](:Mayor) of Peterborough is [Jeff Leal](:Jeff_Leal).Peterborough is known as the gateway to the [Kawarthas](:Kawartha_lakes_%28Ontario%29), \"cottage country\", a large recreational region of the province. It is named in honour of [Peter Robinson](:Peter_Robinson_%281785%E2%80%931838%29), an early Canadian politician who oversaw the first major immigration to the area. The city is the seat of [Peterborough County](:Peterborough_County%2C_Ontario).Peterborough's nickname in the distant past was \"The Electric City\" as it was the first town in Canada to use electric streetlights. It also underscores the historical and present-day importance of technology and manufacturing as an economic base of the city, which has operations from large multi-national companies such as [Siemens](:Siemens), [Rolls-Royce Limited](:Rolls-Royce_Limited), and [General Electric](:General_Electric), and more local businesses such as Merit Precision Ltd., Dynacast and Bryston. Electricity was one of the reasons [Quaker Oats](:Quaker_Oats) moved to the city, and as part of [PepsiCo](:PepsiCo) remains a major fixture in the downtown area. However, over the years the number of major manufacturing plants has declined, and General Electric closed its last remaining facility in 2018. As a result, employment has been shifting toward the service industries and tourism is now the leading industry in the area."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
