<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Hamburg> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Hamburg** (Template:IPA-de, Template:IPA-dedia; Template:Lang-nds Template:IPA-nds, officially the **Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg** (Template:Lang-de; Template:Lang-nds), is the [second-largest city](:List_of_cities_in_Germany_by_population) in [Germany](:Germany) after [Berlin](:Berlin), as well as the overall [7th largest city](:List_of_cities_in_the_European_Union_by_population_within_city_limits) and largest non-[capital city](:Capital_city) in the [European Union](:European_Union) with a population of over 1.85 million. Hamburg's urban area has a population of around 2.5 million and is part of the [Hamburg Metropolitan Region](:Hamburg_Metropolitan_Region), which has a population of over 5.1 million people in total. The city lies on the [River Elbe](:River_Elbe) and two of its [tributaries](:Tributaries), the [River Alster](:River_Alster) and the [River Bille](:Bille_%28Elbe%29). One of Germany's 16 [federated states](:States_of_Germany), Hamburg is surrounded by [Schleswig-Holstein](:Schleswig-Holstein) to the north and [Lower Saxony](:Lower_Saxony) to the south.The official name reflects [Hamburg's history](:History_of_Hamburg) as a member of the medieval [Hanseatic League](:Hanseatic_League) and a [free imperial city](:Free_imperial_city) of the [Holy Roman Empire](:Holy_Roman_Empire). Before the 1871 [unification of Germany](:Unification_of_Germany), it was a fully [sovereign](:Sovereignty) [city state](:City_state), and before 1919 formed a civic republic [headed constitutionally](:Constitutional_monarchy) by a class of hereditary [grand burghers](:Grand_burgher) or *[Hanseaten](:Hanseaten_%28class%29)*. Beset by disasters such as the [Great Fire of Hamburg](:Great_Fire_of_Hamburg), [North Sea flood of 1962](:North_Sea_flood_of_1962) and military conflicts including [World War II bombing raids](:Bombing_of_Hamburg_in_World_War_II), the city has managed to recover and emerge wealthier after each catastrophe.Hamburg is Europe's [third largest port](:List_of_busiest_ports_in_Europe), after [Rotterdam](:Rotterdam) and [Antwerp](:Antwerp). Major regional broadcaster [NDR](:Norddeutscher_Rundfunk), the printing and publishing firm [Gruner + Jahr](:Gruner_%2B_Jahr) and the newspapers *[Der Spiegel](:Der_Spiegel)* and *[Die Zeit](:Die_Zeit)* are based in the city. Hamburg is the seat of Germany's oldest [stock exchange](:Hamburg_Stock_Exchange) and the [world's oldest merchant bank](:List_of_oldest_banks_in_continuous_operation), [Berenberg Bank](:Berenberg_Bank). Media, commercial, logistical, and industrial firms with significant locations in the city include multinationals [Airbus](:Airbus), [Blohm + Voss](:Blohm_%2B_Voss), [Aurubis](:Aurubis), [Beiersdorf](:Beiersdorf), and [Unilever](:Unilever). Hamburg is also a major European [science, research, and education hub](:Education_in_Hamburg), with several universities and institutions. The city enjoys a very high quality of living, being ranked 19th in the 2019 Mercer Quality of Living Survey.Hamburg hosts specialists in world economics and international law, including [consular and diplomatic missions](:List_of_diplomatic_missions_in_Hamburg) as the [International Tribunal for the Law of the Sea](:International_Tribunal_for_the_Law_of_the_Sea), the [EU-LAC Foundation](:EU-LAC_Foundation), and the [UNESCO Institute for Lifelong Learning](:UNESCO_Institute_for_Lifelong_Learning), multipartite international political conferences and summits such as [Europe and China](:Hamburg_Summit%3A_China_meets_Europe) and the [G20](:2017_G20_Hamburg_summit). Both former German chancellors [Helmut Schmidt](:Helmut_Schmidt) and [Angela Merkel](:Angela_Merkel) were born in Hamburg. The former [Mayor of Hamburg](:List_of_mayors_of_Hamburg), [Olaf Scholz](:Olaf_Scholz), has been the current German chancellor since December 2021.Hamburg is a major international and domestic [tourist destination](:Hamburg%23Tourism). The *[Speicherstadt](:Speicherstadt)* and *[Kontorhausviertel](:Kontorhaus_District)* were declared [World Heritage Sites](:World_Heritage_Site) by [UNESCO](:UNESCO) in 2015. Hamburg's rivers and canals are crossed by around 2,500 bridges, making it the city with the highest number of bridges in Europe. Aside from its rich architectural heritage, the city is also home to notable cultural venues such as the [Elbphilharmonie](:Elbphilharmonie) and [Laeiszhalle](:Laeiszhalle) concert halls. It gave birth to movements like *[Hamburger Schule](:Hamburger_Schule)* and paved the way for bands including [the Beatles](:The_Beatles_in_Hamburg). Hamburg is also known for several [theatres](:List_of_theatres_in_Hamburg) and a variety of musical shows. [St. Pauli](:St%2E_Pauli)'s [Reeperbahn](:Reeperbahn) is among the best-known European entertainment districts."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
